# The settings that live in Bitwarden, one name per line. Everything else the
# backend reads is committed, in common.env and <environment>.env next to this
# file. The deploy refuses a Bitwarden entry not listed here, and a committed
# setting that is.
#
# A secret here is anything that unlocks something, plus the half of a pair that
# is generated and rotated with it (JWT_PUBLIC_KEY, AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID).
# This repository is public: a value committed by mistake must be rotated, not
# just deleted.
DATABASE_URL
MIGRATIONS_DATABASE_URL
READ_REPLICA_URL
REDIS_URL
JWT_PRIVATE_KEY
JWT_PUBLIC_KEY
JWT_PUBLIC_KEY_OLD
URL_TOKEN_SECRET
URL_TOKEN_SECRET_OLD
ENCRYPTION_KEY
ENCRYPTION_KEY_OLD
PASSWORD_PEPPER
PASSWORD_PEPPER_OLD
METRICS_TOKEN
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET
RESEND_API_KEY
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
VIRUSTOTAL_API_KEY
# Not a credential, but anyone holding it can fill the Sentry project with noise.
SENTRY_DSN
